<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mohammad Shah Qajar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1808–1848</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Mohammad Shah</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mohammad Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> was shah of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> from 1834 to 1848, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath Ali Shah</a> and son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a>. Guided by his minister Haji Mirza Aqasi, he besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herat">Herat</a> in 1837–1838 but withdrew under British pressure after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharg-island">Kharg Island</a>. His reign also saw the rise of the Babi movement, whose founder, the Bab, was imprisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mohammad-shah-qajar.jpg" alt="Mohammad Shah Qajar" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OnlyDeductiveReasoning · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mohammad_Shah_Qajar_(r.1834-1848)_by_Abu%27l-Hasan_Sani_al-Mulk_(1814%E2%80%931866).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
